\f1\fs22 Chip Stockwell wasn\'92t passed over for promotion fifteen times; he refused every time.\
\pard\pardeftab720\fi360\sl220\qj\partightenfactor0
\cf2 <$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>He was a kiln of a man: cheeks red as bricks, hair red as fire, eyes amber as ale. He lumbered as if he might crumble at any moment if not for the overstretched balloon of his overalls holding him together. His pockets groaned, his buttons screeched, and his plumber\'92s crack grinned at anyone unlucky enough to witness it.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>He loathed the very notion of office work, with the distress of a cat in a creek. He\'92d have sooner chosen a coffin than the drab embrace of cheese-grey partitions surrounding a flat-pack stand-up desk, a drooping jaundiced lamp, and a radiation-spewing computer monitor. Knowing an office was inescapable for any higher-ranking cog in the wheel, he staunchly rejected every offer. As each parade of overseers grew more insistent, strident, desperate, and psychasthenic, Chip devised ever more creative schemes to sabotage his own prospects, yet never so blatantly as to risk reassignment. Outright dismissal was off the table: as a certified member of the Global Stasis Technicians Association, he was privy to far too many secrets.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>So he discovered the optimal method for career activism: antagonising the People & Culture Department. They deserved it, a gaggle of officious gatekeepers, trained in nothing more than the gate-keeping of employee salary expectations, and psychological profiling of teddy bears.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>One time, Chip applied for five months\'92 leave in lieu of untaken tea breaks. On winning this privilege, he immediately requested to exchange the benefit for computer equipment. Once the new tech arrived, he systematically sabotaged it, raising vexatious support tickets just to harass IT.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>He abhorred a desk job for one simple reason: he loved his role as a field agent. There wasn\'92t another job in the world that set a lower bar for skills while bestowing the power to arrest any homemaker, including their spouses, children, nannies, and live-in grannies, who dared tamper with company products.\
\pard\pardeftab720\fi360\sl220\qj\partightenfactor0

\f2\fs24 \cf0 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \
\pard\pardeftab720\fi360\sl220\qj\partightenfactor0

\f1\fs22 \cf2 \expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Chip puffed like a blacksmith\'92s bellows as he hauled his industrial carcass up two flights of stairs, passing Unit 15 on the way to Unit 17, where he knocked on the door like an avalanche of bush rock. Conversation and domestic noises from within ceased, as if a broadcaster had been switched off. Presently, the door opened and exposed a meekly terrified woman standing compliant, as if awaiting sentence in a dock. In one hand, she held a spray-bottle of detergent, and in the other, an absorbent rag.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93Missus Chuglingham?\'94 Chip asked through acid-washed vocal chords.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>The woman spasmed a resigned nod as she looked to the floor and polished Chip\'92s boots with her gaze.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93I\'92m Chip Stockwell, Stasis Anomalies Investigator from Fridge Force, ma\'92am.\'94 Chip tilted the badge clipped to his breast pocket, as if he might blind Missus Chuglingham with reflected light. She flinched. \'93There\'92s been a complaint,\'94 he said, and checked his clip board. \'93A smell.\'94 His plumber\'92s butt-crack itched, so he scratched it.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Missus Chuglingham blushed with shame. \'93Yes. We\'92re terribly sorry. You see, we\'92ve been on vacation\'85 for three weeks!\'85 and when we returned, only this morning\'85 well\'85\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Chip sniffed the air. \'93Did you leave perishables out of your stasis unit?\'92 he asked, referring to the alternative appliances that substituted for refrigerators in this mouldy universe.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Most technology in this universe was more backward than in any other. In the few advanced cases, such tech was always weaponised for such horrifically evil purposes, it would have been better if it never existed. \
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Take the Grayling Corp Home Defence Kettle\'99, a countertop appliance that promised to boil water \'93from ambient to eruption\'94 in under three seconds. Ostensibly designed to deter intruders, its retractable anti-personnel stinger/squirter was equipped with enough capsaicin to drop any trespasser south of a cassowary. But defence is all about the software, and Grayling's patented ThreatID\'99 Facial Recognition System (coded by the same team responsible for supermarket self-checkout security and arrest systems) remained obstinately confused about the categories \'93pet\'94 vs \'93pest.\'94 In ideal conditions, it could distinguish a neighbour from a burglar, or a spouse from a telemarketer, though extensive testing suggested it was best at boiling the family budgie.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Stasis units were the greatest leap forward in food safety in the entire multiverse, with one small flaw: they didn\'92t actually make anything hot or cold. Temperature? Not their problem. Which is why every kitchen still bristled with a conventional chilling fridge for beer, ice cream, and\'97if you were into that sort of thing\'97bracingly invigorating underwear.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Naturally, this spawned cultural oddities. Chief among them: The Knights of the Blessed Order of the Blown Kleenex, whose annual Cough-It-Up Pageant drew cranky geezers from hundreds of kilometres around. Not content with exchanging anecdotes over cheap bourbon, they began preserving their most revolting expectorants, competing for the coveted Golden Tissue Box. All was well and sickening until Pageant the Fifth, when a veteran entered a specimen that had mutated into an organism so virulent it proved immediately fatal, dissolving the eyes, duodenum, and left kneecap of every host. It even killed brown dogs. Within an hour, the infected could be seen blindly limping in circles as their insides imploded and their dignity evaporated.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Let that be a lesson for us all: no matter how beneficent a technology looks, never store your snot in a stasis unit.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93Did you leave perishables out of your stasis unit?\'94 Chip repeated, less kindly, though, in fairness, he was never kind.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93No\'85 there\'92s something wrong\'85\'94 Missus Chuglingham swallowed hard and blanched, chewing her lip and sagging her shoulders. \'93With the stasis unit.\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Chip straightened, puffing out his chest. \'93Ma\'92am, that\'92s quite impossible.\'94 They always tried to blame the stasis unit. But the stasis unit was perfect. This was serious business: Chip couldn\'92t afford to believe her story. \'93I must inspect the unit. Please stand aside.\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Missus Chuglingham hesitated, just long enough for Chip to justify pulling his weapon, which, in any other universe, would have looked comical: a stubby popgun with a plunger jammed down the muzzle.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93You don\'92t want me to glue you to the wall, ma\'92am,\'94 Chip warned, sneering behind his limp gunsight, picturing her hopeless and squirming, glued to plaster.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>From inside, a metallic clang of pots falling punctuated the tension. Missus Chuglingham gasped, covered her mouth, squeezed her eyelids shut against tears, and ran down the hallway. Chip loped in pursuit, into the kitchen, where he found a man scooping ruined food into a garbage sack.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93Step away from the vegetables,\'94 Chip ordered. He fished a respiratory mask from his pocket and looped it over his ears.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>The man did as told, rubber-gloved hands raised.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Behind the mask, Chip kept his mouth shut and held his breath as he peered into the foul garbage sack. \'93This is evidence-tampering,\'94 Chip accused.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93Is it?\'94 muttered the man.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93You know it is, sir.\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93We\'92ve done nothing wrong,\'94 Mr Chuglingham insisted, pulling his wife close as she tried to brush away flecks of rot. \'93We were away, and we came home to\'85 this!\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Missus Chuglingham barely stifled a retch.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93Nothing wrong, hey?\'94 Chip tapped a black scuff mark with the tip of his boot. \'93Your stasis unit\'92s been moved.\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>The Chuglinghams stared at the mark, appalled. \'93We didn\'92t move it,\'94 Missus said.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Chip grimaced, nodded, peered down his nose at the terrified couple. \'93Let\'92s see what we have.\'94 He wrestled the unit from its alcove; the feet slid easier over the smeared rot on the tiles. He spun it to inspect the back. With all the athleticism of an arthritic macaque, he knelt, peered into the mechanical guts, and froze: the safety seal on the green box had been snapped, and scratch marks ringed the lid.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>With proprietary spanners and screwdrivers, Chip prized loose the box, holding it reverently for a long moment, then exhaled, braced himself, and cracked the lid.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93Holy gravity fuck!\'94 he spat. He glared at the couple. \'93Where is it?\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93Where\'92s what?\'94 Mr. Chuglingham quavered.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93The\'97\'94 Chip began, then choked back the rest, for about three seconds. \'93The disc! The taxing Gyges Disc!\'94 he blurted. Realising the gravity of his outburst, he strode into the hall, noted open doors, counted three bedrooms. \'93Do you have children?\'94 he demanded.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Missus Chuglingham implored, \'93Please! Leave them out of it.\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93How many?\'94 Chip pressed, harder. \'93Two?\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93Two.\'94 Mr. Chuglingham sputtered, a man who knew it was better to cooperate.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93Where are they? School?\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>They were silent.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93Which school?\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93Tregear Public,\'94 Mr. Chuglingham confessed.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Chip fished out his tablet, tapped it. It rang dull as bone. He held it to his ear. \'93I need an immediate collection. Tregear Public School. Chuglingham. Two.\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>While the Chuglinghams whimpered, Chip cuffed them. \'93I\'92m detaining you for violations of the Food Preservation Secrets Act.\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93But we\'92re innocent!\'94 Mr. Chuglingham sobbed.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93You\'92ll get your day in arbitration, after you and your family have served your time in a housekeeping reeducation camp.\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93But those camps are offshore!\'94 Missus Chuglingham wailed.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93That\'92s a matter of national security,\'94 Chip said, entirely without scruple.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93Trying to clean out a stasis unit is national security now?\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93You know it is, ma\'92am.\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Chip called for a back-up team to collect the Chuglinghams. On the way out, he thought he could hear sobbing as he passed Unit 15. He shrugged and thought nothing more of it. Inside, a family grieved the loss of a father and husband who inexplicably committed suicide\'97one Travis Teh.
